## Orientation previews (NOT final model)
- DISSAVED rate by age: 24y=25.5%, 25y=24.1%, 26y=25.8%, 27y=26.3%, 28y=24.1%, 29y=23.3%, 30y=20.2%, 31y=21.6%, 32y=18.2% (declining).
- Centered quadratic logistic: AGE_c b=-0.044 (p<.001), AGE2_c b=-0.009 (p~.03).
- Full logistic: AGE_c b~-0.049 (p<.001), LOGINC b~+0.12 (p<.001), MALE b~-0.20 (p<.001), BLACK b~+0.21 (p<.001), HISPANIC b~+0.10 (ns), BMI ns. Pseudo R2 ~0.006 (weak signal).

## Proposed approach
- Outcome: P(DISSAVED=1) on full sample.
- Age: centered quadratic AGE_c + AGE2_c; test curvature (Wald on AGE2_c) vs linear; per-age rates as robustness.
- Covariates: GENDER, INCOME(log1p), BMI, BLACK, HISPANIC. Exclude SAMESAVE (outcome partition).
- Report: coefficients, OR + 95% CI, average marginal effect of age (per year), predicted P at ages 24/28/32.
- Sensitivity: with/without INCOME (mediator vs confounder); BMI winsorize.

## Key assumptions / uncertainties
- 1989 cross-section => association only, no causality.
- SAMESAVE is a competing/partitioned outcome, not a confounder.
- INCOME likely both mediator and confounder.
- AGE/AGE_2 collinearity resolved by centering.
- Large N => emphasize effect size over p-values.
- Narrow 24-32 window => no extrapolation; weakly identified curvature.
- Time-preference lens: DISSAVED ~ proxy for discounting/patience.
